/* What happens to specific foods and drinks. */
window.NUTRIENTS = [
  ['🍞', 'Carbohydrates', 'Starch and sugars are broken into glucose (plus fructose and galactose). Glucose is the fast fuel for the brain and muscles; extra is stored as glycogen in the liver and muscles, and the rest turned into fat. Fibre isn’t digested but feeds gut bacteria and keeps you regular.', ['mouth', 'smallint', 'liver', 'pancreas', 'muscles']],
  ['🍗', 'Proteins', 'Broken into amino acids in the stomach and small intestine. The body uses them to build and repair muscles, enzymes, hormones and antibodies. Extra amino acids are burned for energy and their nitrogen becomes urea in the liver, removed by the kidneys.', ['stomach', 'smallint', 'liver', 'kidneys', 'muscles']],
  ['🥑', 'Fats', 'Bile breaks fat into droplets and lipase splits it into fatty acids and glycerol. They are repackaged and travel through the lymph into the blood. Fat is a rich energy store, builds cell walls and hormones, and carries vitamins A, D, E and K.', ['gallbladder', 'pancreas', 'smallint', 'lymph']],
  ['🥦', 'Vitamins & minerals', 'Absorbed mostly in the small intestine. Iron builds haemoglobin, calcium builds bones, vitamin C helps skin and immunity, B12 keeps nerves and blood healthy, iodine makes thyroid hormone.', ['smallint', 'liver', 'bones', 'thyroid']],
  ['🌾', 'Fibre', 'Not digested by our enzymes. Soluble fibre (oats, beans) forms a gel that slows sugar absorption and lowers cholesterol; insoluble fibre (whole grains, vegetables) adds bulk. Gut bacteria ferment it into short-chain fatty acids that feed the colon.', ['largeint', 'smallint']],
  ['💧', 'Water', 'Absorbed mostly in the small intestine, the rest in the colon. Most adults need about 1.5–2 litres of drinks a day, more in heat and exercise. The kidneys balance how much you keep.', ['smallint', 'largeint', 'kidneys', 'bladder']],
  ['☕', 'Caffeine (coffee, tea, energy drinks)', 'Absorbed within about 45 minutes. It blocks adenosine, the brain’s “sleepy” signal, so you feel alert; effects last 3–7 hours (half remains after about 5 hours), so afternoon caffeine can spoil sleep. Up to about 400 mg a day (3–4 cups of coffee) is fine for most adults; less in pregnancy and for teenagers.', ['brain', 'heart', 'kidneys']],
  ['🍷', 'Alcohol', 'Absorbed quickly through the stomach and small intestine — even faster on an empty stomach. The liver breaks it down at about one standard drink an hour; the in-between chemical, acetaldehyde, is toxic. Alcohol blocks ADH so you lose water, and in large amounts it depresses the brain and breathing. Less is better for health.', ['stomach', 'liver', 'brain', 'kidneys']],
  ['🥤', 'Sugary drinks', 'The sugar is absorbed very fast, causing a quick rise in blood sugar and insulin. Liquid calories don’t fill you up much, and the fructose is handled by the liver, where too much turns into fat. Acid and sugar also attack teeth.', ['mouth', 'pancreas', 'liver']],
  ['🥛', 'Milk', 'A mix of water, protein, fat, lactose sugar and calcium. Lactose needs the enzyme lactase — many adults worldwide make less of it (lactose intolerance). Milk leaves the stomach more slowly than water.', ['stomach', 'smallint', 'bones']]
];

